{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact clean pushed head a43f6c3f1e6a40a607899e85ce2b2a8a209bef0e (request/declared-input-contract-design, one commit over main 9280be8), sole artifact 59dcae61 notes/2026-09-05-declared-input-contract.md, reviewed from my own detached checkout against Hugh request e919a5d7, the producers at 9280be8 and Inventory 12c1687b. Three blockers, seven should-fixes; the producer table itself is accurate.\n\nVERIFIED. Every row of the producer table matches the code: Tailapp meta is exactly INTEGER position, TEXT event_id, TEXT event_type at internal/projection/projection.go:523 for both stages; the nine envelope fields with four nullable match jsonataddl/dialect.go:167-177 and the producer at projection.go:486-491 where pointerValue yields present-as-null, never absent; record is always a non-null JSON object (internal/ingest/canonical.go:190-210, decoded UseNumber at projection.go:481-485); Inventory's empty non-null meta, six TEXT scalars with position and timestamp as strings, rests_on as a never-null string array and the closed payload{id,sku,qty} match gitseq-inventory internal/recordruntime/input.go:80-88 and dialect.go:17-24; emission_ordinal is fixture-only (docs/reference/ddl-jsonata.md:204). Validating the encoded representation rather than Go values is the right seam, since ValidateValue requires json.Number (values.go:102-119) and the producer's int64 would otherwise be refused; ValidateProgramInput before reads is correctly placed because projection.go:516-522 executes reads before the input exists.\n\nBLOCKER 1, note lines 118-123. The old-storage claim contradicts the implemented continuation path. The note says stored projections with the old runtime refuse continuation under the new handle. In the tree, a dialect change makes the projection upgradePending, internal/engine/engine.go:213 opens it with OpenForUpgrade, which passes allowLegacyRuntime=true and skips the runtime comparison at internal/projection/projection.go:174; mode continue reaches Projection.Continue, whose only gate is ContinueCompatible (internal/profile/compatibility.go:12-29), which compares writable table shapes and never the runtime; projection.go:351 then rewrites runtime_profile to the new digest in place with every row kept. Reset requires an acknowledgement (engine.go:641); continuation does not. Either state that continuation is safe and argue it, or make the ContinueCompatible/Continue change an explicit, owned, gated item. As written this is the silent reinterpretation the conditions exclude.\n\nBLOCKER 2, note line 91. rows is the third member of EvaluationInput (jsonataddl/evaluate.go:24-28) and is a structured host input: projection.go:516-522 runs executeRead and fromSQLite (projection.go:588-591) returns whatever the driver gives through ReadRowValue's default branch (values.go:272-311). Evaluate marshals Rows to JSONata with no validation (evaluate.go:69-76) while every output path is validated. The note disposes of it with existing read-plan rules that constrain which relations are read, not what shape arrives. Bring rows into the declaration (the read plan already names cardinality and columns) or state explicitly that it is out of I5 scope and why; do not assert a protection that does not exist.\n\nBLOCKER 3, note lines 66-73. The fixture prescription cannot be executed. With one closed meta object per dialect and unknown members refused (line 39), eight released corpus inputs carrying meta.emission_ordinal refuse (corpus/v1/basic/inputs/accumulate-first and -prior, and six under corpus/v1/misbehavior/inputs), eight Go call sites passing Meta with position alone refuse (including internal/profile/runtime_test.go:73 and tailapps/embed_test.go:60,73,364), and the public ExampleLoadApplication in jsonataddl/example_test.go:57-61 refuses. The note's three options do not cover this: explicit fixture contracts cannot apply because the corpus loads every case with the production Tailapp dialect (corpus_test.go:80, README:29-30) and substituting a fixture dialect defeats the freeze; padding is forbidden; weakening the constructor is forbidden. The obvious disposition, deleting the vestigial key that was never a producer field, is not offered, and the public Example is not mentioned. Name the disposition per fixture class and say what the released Example becomes.\n\nSHOULD-FIX. (S1, lines 82-84) three overlapping number sentences contradict: reject malformed or non-finite numbers is unqualified, so an OTLP record containing 1e999, legal JSON that survives UseNumber today, would start refusing; scope it to declared INTEGER/REAL members or state the behaviour change. (S2, line 84) the declared depth bound silently reuses Limits.MaxDepth, whose one use is JSONata evaluation depth (compile.go:460); name a new limit or state the consequence for records nested deeper than 64. (S3, lines 108-110) the nine identity components are asserted, never enumerated; list them from identity.go:21-31 and say which two change. (S4, lines 107-108) only one dialect is built into the core, Tailapp() at dialect.go:159; GitseqRecord() lives in the consumer on its own release cycle, and gate 3 does not name the gitseq-record version bump. (S5, line 48) the presence flag's polarity is unstated; a Required bool inverts the existing Nullable zero-is-strict convention and would make all six Inventory fields optional silently; name it so the zero value stays strict. (S6, line 111) the corpus records no composed digest (corpus_test.go:24 hardcodes the runtime profile literal); the fixture that moves is internal/profile/runtime_test.go:36-37. (S7, lines 127-134) gate 1's condition restates the work with no observable pass/fail, and docs/reference/ddl-jsonata.md:174-205 and otel-records.md:13-26, which already publish the input shape, are neither cited nor assigned to a gate.\n\nNOTES. projection.go:411 writes gap_position and gap_reason outside the rolled-back transaction, so line 98 is narrower than it reads. Declaring Inventory's payload in the core duplicates recordruntime/input.go:65-72; say whether the host check narrows or stays as belt-and-braces. The fixed input-semantics version inside the dialect encoding (lines 110-111) is redundant with the core.interface bump.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected Tailapp layers: jsonataddl (dialect.go, evaluate.go, application.go, values.go, compile.go:103-107, identity.go), internal/profile, internal/projection producers and continuation, internal/engine activation, corpus/v1, and docs/reference/ddl-jsonata.md and otel-records.md; consumer layers internal/recordruntime and docs/reference/input-contract.md. This head changes no contract: one note under notes/, no code, gates green; design-only honoured. The design as written implies an unstated change to the activation/continuation boundary (blocker 1), the one boundary it does not name.\n\nSECURITY. The design closes a live ambiguity path: EnvelopeField.Type is validated nowhere (compile.go:103-107 checks only the name) and flows unescaped into Canonical at dialect.go:220, so a crafted Type string can inject canonical lines and collide two dialects onto one digest; ComposeIdentity's separator rejection (identity.go:63) inspects the hashed value, not the pre-image. The note's type admission (43-44) and JSON escaping (100-103) each close it; say so, so the implementer writes the collision mutant. Low severity today since dialects are compiled-in Go values. Post-marshal validation avoids json.Number type confusion. Left to later and must not be: storage treatment (blocker 1) and rows (blocker 2). Correctly deferred: evaluator work and allocation bounds, matching recordruntime/dialect.go:8-10.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Drop the fixed input-semantics version from the dialect encoding, core.interface already carries it; collapse lines 82-84 into one rule scoped to declared members; lines 113-116 restate 31-33.\n\nGATES at a43f6c3f from the detached checkout: git diff --check clean; go build and go vet clean; go test ./... all 14 packages ok; jsonataddl module tests ok with GOWORK=off; scripts/check-jsonataddl-boundary.sh rc 0; no Makefile or docs gate exists in this repository.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:59dcae618107ba581c3a235c6851796518d29531","head":"a43f6c3f1e6a40a607899e85ce2b2a8a209bef0e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:e526d0ee80518883ebbeb010922c8b5ace7a0c7b\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:e526d0ee80518883ebbeb010922c8b5ace7a0c7b","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:59dcae618107ba581c3a235c6851796518d29531, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:a5448e19df7c6ba6021d30fd3f5349e64f268082, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:e526d0ee80518883ebbeb010922c8b5ace7a0c7b stale; retired bases: git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:0a38cf2bea5866a24fc1eb6fdb211b4fac7e09a4, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:10d2c0befea9aab23a3c6ca1b36323c42cb49225, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:19f04c4080403c78bf4b5161d9068eb983fc9fcf, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:1fb485d3c0f06ebac3bd50c0771285f773d283dd and 34 more","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:0a38cf2bea5866a24fc1eb6fdb211b4fac7e09a4, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:10d2c0befea9aab23a3c6ca1b36323c42cb49225, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:19f04c4080403c78bf4b5161d9068eb983fc9fcf, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:1fb485d3c0f06ebac3bd50c0771285f773d283dd and 34 more","verdict":"changes-requested"}}